    (v_course_id, v_level, 'The Operating System, Which Is Most of the Job', 'The existing lessons cover what the role is and what hardware is inside a machine. Almost every ticket you will ever handle, though, is a software problem — so this is the operating system, from Google''s own IT Support Certificate.', v_module_offset + 1)
  returning id into v_m1;

  insert into public.lessons (module_id, title, description, youtube_url, order_number, learning_objectives, notes, practice_activity, knowledge_check) values
    (v_m1, 'What an Operating System Actually Does', 'Google''s IT Support Certificate on how an operating system is put together and what it manages. About 36 minutes — long, so plan a sitting for it.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7CrfA-ukRe4', 1,
      array['Describe what the kernel, drivers and user space each do', 'Explain how the OS manages memory, processes and hardware', 'Use that model to reason about why a machine is slow or unresponsive'],
      'This lesson runs about 36 minutes and it is worth the data. Everything you will be asked to fix — a slow laptop, a printer that will not print, an application that crashes — is the operating system doing something you can understand once you know how it is built.

A NOTE ON LENGTH before you start: this and the next three lessons come from Google''s IT Support Certificate and each runs half an hour or more. On a mobile bundle that is real money. Download over Wi-Fi where you can, and treat one lesson as one sitting rather than trying to do the module in an evening.

WHAT AN OPERATING SYSTEM IS: the layer between the hardware and everything a person actually uses. It decides which program gets the processor, how memory is shared, how files are stored, and how a keyboard or printer is spoken to.

THE PARTS THAT MATTER FOR SUPPORT WORK:

THE KERNEL is the core. It talks to hardware and allocates resources. When people say a machine has "frozen", something has usually gone wrong between an application and the kernel.

DRIVERS are the translators between the OS and a specific piece of hardware. An enormous share of real support tickets — printers, webcams, Wi-Fi adapters, external monitors — come down to a missing, outdated or wrong driver. Learning to check Device Manager on Windows will resolve more tickets than any other single skill at this level.

PROCESSES are running programs. Task Manager on Windows and Activity Monitor on macOS show them, with what each is using. "The computer is slow" is answered here: one process eating the processor, or memory exhausted and the machine swapping to disk.

MEMORY MANAGEMENT is why a machine with too little RAM slows to a crawl rather than simply refusing — it starts using the disk as overflow, which is dramatically slower.

USER ACCOUNTS AND PERMISSIONS decide who may change what. A great many "it will not let me" tickets are a permissions issue, not a fault.

AND KNOW ALL THREE FAMILIES. Windows dominates offices, macOS shows up in design and media, Linux runs most servers. A Kenyan help desk will mostly be Windows, but the concepts here are the same everywhere, which is exactly why the certificate teaches them this way.',
      'On any computer you can use, open Task Manager (Ctrl+Shift+Esc on Windows) or Activity Monitor. Sort by CPU and note the top three processes; then sort by memory and do the same. Write down what is actually consuming the machine. Then open Device Manager (Windows) or System Information (macOS) and find one device and its driver. Being able to reach both screens without hunting is a real, employable skill.',
      '[{"question":"A user says their printer stopped working after a Windows update. What is the most likely cause at this level?","options":["A driver problem — the translator between the OS and that hardware","The printer''s power supply has failed","The kernel has been corrupted","The user''s account was deleted"],"correct_index":0},{"question":"Why does a machine with too little RAM become extremely slow rather than simply stopping?","options":["The OS starts using the disk as overflow memory, which is far slower","The processor reduces its speed to protect itself","The kernel shuts down background drivers","The network adapter throttles the connection"],"correct_index":0}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m1, 'The Command Line, and Why Support Staff Use It', 'Google''s IT Support Certificate on using the command line — the tool that separates someone who clicks around from someone who fixes things quickly. About 33 minutes.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xg_1UjZqMdQ', 2,
      array['Navigate the filesystem and manage files from a terminal', 'Explain why the command line is faster and more reliable for support tasks', 'Read a command someone gives you before running it'],
      'About 33 minutes, and the single most employable half hour in this course. Everyone applying for a help-desk job says they are "good with computers". Being comfortable at a command line is what makes that claim checkable.

WHY IT MATTERS IN SUPPORT, concretely:
- It works over a remote connection when a desktop is too slow to use
- It does the same thing to two hundred machines through a script
- It reaches diagnostics that have no window — network configuration, running services, system logs
- It is exactly what the person senior to you will tell you to type

THE IDEAS THAT TRANSFER between Windows and Linux even though the commands differ: you are always somewhere in the filesystem; commands take options that change their behaviour; and output can be piped from one command into another.

COMMANDS WORTH KNOWING ON DAY ONE. On Windows: `ipconfig` for network settings, `ping` to test whether something is reachable, `tasklist` for running processes, `sfc /scannow` to check system files. On Linux and macOS: `ls`, `cd`, `pwd`, `cat`, `ping`, `top`, and `df -h` for disk space full.

THE HABIT THAT MATTERS MOST, and it is a safety habit: READ A COMMAND BEFORE YOU RUN IT. The command line does exactly what you say, immediately, with no confirmation dialogue and no undo. Deleting the wrong directory is instant and permanent. Never paste a command from a forum or a chatbot into a production machine without understanding each part of it — this is how real damage gets done by well-meaning junior staff, and it is also the difference between being trusted with access and not.

BE ESPECIALLY CAREFUL with anything containing a wildcard, anything run with administrator or `sudo` rights, and anything that formats or deletes. If you are unsure, ask. Nobody has ever been sacked for asking; people have been sacked for the other thing.

AND WRITE DOWN WHAT YOU RAN. When a fix works, the command you used is worth more than the memory of it — that note becomes your own runbook, and a support person with a runbook is fast.',
      'Open Command Prompt or Terminal and do five things: find where you are, list what is there, move into another folder, check your network settings (`ipconfig` or `ifconfig`), and ping a website to see whether it responds. Write each command and its result in a notes file. That file is the beginning of the runbook you will use in an actual job.',
      '[{"question":"Why is reading a command before running it a safety habit rather than a preference?","options":["The command line executes immediately, with no confirmation and no undo","Commands are logged and reviewed by employers","Typing errors damage the keyboard buffer","The terminal cannot display long commands"],"correct_index":0},{"question":"Why does a support technician use the command line when a desktop interface exists?","options":["It works remotely, scales to many machines and reaches diagnostics that have no window","It uses less electricity","Desktop interfaces cannot manage files","It is the only way to install software"],"correct_index":0}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (module_id, order_number) do nothing;

