  insert into public.lessons (module_id, title, description, youtube_url, order_number, learning_objectives, notes, practice_activity, knowledge_check) values
    (v_m2, 'How Computers Communicate in a Network', 'Google''s IT Support Certificate on networking fundamentals — how one machine reaches another. About 41 minutes; plan a full sitting.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z_hU2zm4_S8', 1,
      array['Explain what an IP address, a MAC address and a port are for', 'Describe the path a request takes from a computer to a server', 'Work through a connectivity problem in a sensible order'],
      'About 41 minutes. Networking is the subject that intimidates beginners most and it is mostly a small number of ideas repeated.

THE ADDRESSES, and why there are several:
- A MAC ADDRESS identifies a physical network adapter. It does not change and it only matters on the local network.
- An IP ADDRESS identifies a machine on a network and can change. Your laptop has a private one on the office network and shares a public one with everyone else behind that router.
- A PORT identifies which service on that machine — web traffic on 80 and 443, email on others. One address, many doors.

WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU OPEN A WEBSITE, in order, because this order IS your troubleshooting order: your machine looks up the name through DNS to get an address; it sends the request to your router; the router forwards it out through your provider; it crosses the internet to the server; the reply comes back the same way.

SO WHEN SOMEONE SAYS "THE INTERNET IS DOWN", work along that path rather than guessing:
1. Is the cable in, or is Wi-Fi actually connected?
2. Does the machine have a sensible IP address? An address starting 169.254 means it never got one from the router — that is a DHCP problem, and it is a common answer.
3. Can you ping the router?
4. Can you ping an outside address, like 8.8.8.8? If that works but names do not resolve, it is DNS — which is by far the most common cause of "the internet is broken" while the connection is fine.
5. Is it one machine or everyone? That single question separates a device problem from a network problem, and asking it first saves an hour.

THIS ORDERED APPROACH IS THE ACTUAL SKILL, and it is worth stating plainly because the video teaches the concepts rather than the method: identify the problem, ask what changed, form one theory, test that theory, then fix it and write down what it was. Changing five things at once and finding it works teaches you nothing and is not repeatable.

A KENYAN NOTE THE COURSE DOES NOT COVER: on a mobile-data or router-shared connection, "slow internet" is often a bundle that has run out or been throttled rather than a fault. Check the data balance before you rebuild anyone''s network settings.',
      'On your own machine, find your IP address, your router''s address (the default gateway) and your DNS servers. Then walk the path deliberately: ping your router, ping 8.8.8.8, then ping a website by name. Note which succeed. Finally, do it once while deliberately disconnected, and watch exactly which step fails first — that is what a real fault looks like.',
      '[{"question":"A machine can ping 8.8.8.8 but cannot open any website by name. What is the problem?","options":["DNS — the connection works but names are not resolving to addresses","The network cable is unplugged","The router has failed","The machine has no IP address"],"correct_index":0},{"question":"What is the most useful first question when someone reports the internet is down?","options":["Is it just this machine, or is everyone affected?","Which browser are you using?","When did you last restart?","What is your password?"],"correct_index":0}]'::jsonb),
    (v_m2, 'How Devices Connect to the Internet', 'Google''s IT Support Certificate on the connection itself — routers, providers and what sits between a device and the wider internet. About 47 minutes, so watch it in two parts.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JgxUD5X_Pe4', 2,
      array['Describe the equipment between a device and the internet and what each does', 'Explain what a router does that a switch does not', 'Diagnose whether a fault is inside the building or with the provider'],
      'This one runs about 47 minutes — longer than any other lesson here. WATCH IT IN TWO SITTINGS, and download it over Wi-Fi if you can, because on a mobile bundle a video this long is a real cost.

THE EQUIPMENT, and what each piece actually does:
- A SWITCH connects devices within one local network and passes traffic between them.
- A ROUTER connects one network to another — in practice, your network to the internet. It is the boundary, which is why so much troubleshooting happens at it.
- A MODEM converts between the signal your provider sends and something the network understands. In most homes and small offices these are one box, which is why people call the whole thing "the router".
- AN ACCESS POINT is the Wi-Fi radio. Often the same box again.

NAT AND PRIVATE ADDRESSES: everything inside your network has a private address, and the router translates them to share one public address. This is why every device in the building appears to the outside world as the same address, and why you cannot reach a machine inside a network directly from outside without deliberately configuring it.

WHERE IS THE FAULT — inside or outside? This is the question your employer actually needs answered, because one you fix and the other you report:
- Can devices reach each other on the local network, but nothing external? The problem is at the router or beyond.
- Is it only Wi-Fi devices, while a cabled machine works? The access point or the wireless configuration.
- Do the router''s own status lights indicate no service? That is the provider, and the correct action is to log it, not to reconfigure anything.

WI-FI PROBLEMS ARE OFTEN PHYSICS, not configuration. Distance, walls, and a neighbourhood full of routers on the same channel all degrade a connection that is set up perfectly. Moving the access point higher and away from metal and microwaves genuinely helps.

AND FOR THE KENYAN CONTEXT: many small offices run on a router with a SIM, or share a connection over a mobile hotspot, and power cuts are a real and frequent cause of "the network is down" — the equipment has restarted and something did not come back. Check power and check the provider''s status before you touch a setting. Writing down the working configuration BEFORE a power cut is a small habit that saves whole mornings.',
      'Find the router on any network you are allowed to look at. Note its make, its address, and what its status lights indicate. Then answer three questions in writing: can two devices on this network reach each other, can they reach the internet, and does anything change when you use a cable instead of Wi-Fi? That three-step check is the shape of a real diagnosis, and it tells you whether you are fixing this or phoning the provider.',
      '[{"question":"What does a router do that a switch does not?","options":["Connect one network to another — it is the boundary between your network and the internet","Provide Wi-Fi to wireless devices","Assign MAC addresses to devices","Convert electrical signals into light"],"correct_index":0},{"question":"Devices on the local network can reach each other but nothing outside. Where is the fault?","options":["At the router or beyond it, with the provider","On each individual device","In the network switch","In the DNS settings of one machine"],"correct_index":0}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (module_id, order_number) do nothing;

